00:07:00.027 --> 00:07:05.298
pop contracts. Forecasts that are too sure and how researchers measure that gap

00:07:05.298 --> 00:07:10.578
is where how it works starts. Overprecision in a 2013 paper in psychological

00:07:10.578 --> 00:07:15.678
science is excessive confidence that you know the truth. Those authors did not

00:07:15.678 --> 00:07:19.718
take a spoken confidence band at face value. They watched how little a

00:07:19.718 --> 00:07:23.978
person moved a point estimate once the cost of guessing too high differed from the cost

00:07:23.978 --> 00:07:29.118
of guessing too low. Picture the penalty for overshooting as the heavier one. Someone

00:07:29.118 --> 00:07:33.778
who is genuinely unsure should slide the guest toward the cheaper mistake, and a bigger

00:07:33.778 --> 00:07:38.738
slide should mean a wider uncertainty. The paper compared the precision implied by that

00:07:38.738 --> 00:07:43.598
slide with what the person actually knew. People moved less than their knowledge justified.

00:07:44.558 --> 00:07:48.738
Subjective confidence drove the shortfall. They behaved as if the right answer lived

00:07:48.738 --> 00:07:53.598
in a tighter band than their record supported. A different pile-up shows up when the forecast

00:07:53.598 --> 00:07:59.518
is a probability across models. A 2020 paper, when our Bayesian model probabilities over

00:07:59.518 --> 00:08:05.338
confident, finds that a posterior often concentrates on one model even when other measures of fit

00:08:05.338 --> 00:08:11.358
or a forecasting ability show no strong preference. A moderate change in the sample can then move

00:08:11.358 --> 00:08:16.698
that concentration onto a different model. The authors derive the sampling variance of the Bayes

00:08:16.698 --> 00:08:19.738
factor in univariate and multivariate linear regression.

00:08:20.498 --> 00:08:24.478
Averaging several experts does not, by itself, fix the first problem.

00:08:24.618 --> 00:08:29.738
A 2012 paper in the Journal of Modeling and Management compared Cook's Classical Model,

00:08:30.258 --> 00:08:35.358
Equal Weight Linear Pulling, and the single Best Expert on a large database of real-world

00:08:35.358 --> 00:08:38.298
judgments, with leave one-out cross-validation.

00:08:38.798 --> 00:08:42.958
Both pulling methods beat the best expert, so more than one voice helped.

00:08:42.958 --> 00:08:47.258
Linear pooling still did not effectively counteract expert overconfidence.

00:08:47.718 --> 00:08:52.218
Cook's model was more sensitive to experts leaning on one another than equal weights were,

00:08:52.678 --> 00:08:57.618
and high dependence generally produced much poorer aggregated results, while equal weights

00:08:57.618 --> 00:08:59.618
held up better across dependence levels.

00:09:00.238 --> 00:09:03.918
Broadening subjective intervals may occasionally soften overconfidence.

00:09:04.438 --> 00:09:09.258
That paper says, and the number of experts and the number of seed questions also change

00:09:09.258 --> 00:09:11.158
how well the aggregate is calibrated.

00:09:11.158 --> 00:09:16.798
calling all of that one trait is the dispute. A 2011 paper in the Journal of

00:09:16.798 --> 00:09:20.878
Behavioral Decision Making found that miscalibration on an interval production

00:09:20.878 --> 00:09:26.218
task correlated across two general knowledge sets given at different times which

00:09:26.218 --> 00:09:30.798
looks like a trait. Harder questions brought more over precision and at the same

00:09:30.798 --> 00:09:35.738
time, more under placement of one's rank against other people. Dispositional

00:09:35.738 --> 00:09:40.578
optimism and self-efficacy did not track that interval miss. Measures of

00:09:40.578 --> 00:09:45.818
tendency toward positive illusions correlated with each other, failed to correlate with the interval

00:09:45.818 --> 00:09:52.018
task, and correlated negatively with optimism about societal risks. In a final study, that same

00:09:52.018 --> 00:09:57.158
tendency did predict miscalibration when the same questions were scored by asking people to state

00:09:57.158 --> 00:10:03.118
probabilities. The author's conclusion is that overconfidence is a series of overlapping constructs,

00:10:03.238 --> 00:10:09.338
not one thing. A calibration chart is the picture of that gap. Stated probability sits on one

00:10:09.338 --> 00:10:14.498
access. The share of those statements that later came true sits on the other. If prices

00:10:14.498 --> 00:10:19.418
near the top of the scale resolved yes about as often as the price implied. The points

00:10:19.418 --> 00:10:25.138
would fall near the diagonal. Overprecision is the bend off that line. High-stated chances

00:10:25.138 --> 00:10:30.458
come true less often than the price suggests, and low-stated chances come true more often.

00:10:31.018 --> 00:10:35.598
None of these papers tests a named venue, and none of them marks a particular contract

00:10:35.598 --> 00:10:41.318
as too high or too low. A price that has rushed towards certainty is still a claim about frequency.

00:10:41.858 --> 00:10:47.078
It has to be checked against how often similarly sure prices on that kind of question were right,

00:10:47.538 --> 00:10:52.298
and against whether the people behind it were independent or were all updating on the same story.

